text:
noah: quick brain dump: we've tried moving eviction credits eagerly but runs into races when donors crash mid-move. thinking about an escrow pattern: credits are reserved in an escrow ledger and only committed to the recipient after a two-phase ack. avoids double-spend and makes donor-crash recovery deterministic. thoughts?

tara gomez: escrow sounds safe but adds extra state + complexity. what's the failure recovery path if the escrow coordinator dies?

noah: plan is fully decentralized: escrow entries are put into the slab-local WAL. coordinator is a leaseholder per-slab; if coordinator dies another node with latest WAL takes over and can either commit or roll back based on tombstone. no central service.

kevin park: two questions: 1) latency impact for small moves (we currently do sub-ms local ops), 2) correctness vs long cursors.

noah: for latency we make escrow optimistic: donor marks credits as "reserved" but still services in-flight reads until commit deadline. commit is async; recipient can start using tentative credits only after commit ack. that keeps common-case fast.

leo wang: long cursor problem again. If a tenant has pinned cursors spanning > commit-deadline we could accidentally demote them. add a "cursor_hold" check to prevent moving credits away until active_cursor_count below threshold. cheap and safe.

maya singh: let's not forget QoS tiers. premium tenants should be excluded from automatic rebalances and escrow moves unless overload emergency. we need policy flags: rebalancer=allowed/opt-in/opt-out per-tenant.

kevin park: what about double-apply if donor crashes after commit but before WAL flush? need idempotency keys on moves.

noah: agreed. each move has a monotonic move_id (slab_epoch + local_counter + tenant_id). apply is idempotent by move_id. WAL record looks like: "reserve(move_id, amount) -> commit(move_id) -> ack(move_id)". recovery scans WAL to complete in-flight move sequences.

tara gomez: metrics and debugging: expose per-tenant metric "escrow_pending", "escrow_committed", "escrow_aborted" and an SLI for stuck escrow entries older than X. also a debug endpoint to list escrow entries with reasons.

leo wang: ops request: add a hardened kill-switch that immediately aborts new escrow creation (freeze) and drains pending escrows. ops runbook should include steps to rollback partial moves by replaying WAL in read-only mode to surface divergent state.

noah: sketch of protocol (pseudocode):
```
// donor slab
reserve(move_id, tenant, amount) {
  if (!slab.can_reweight(tenant)) return DEFER;
  wal.append({move_id, tenant, amount, state: RESERVED});
  slab.credits -= amount; // tentative
  return OK;
}
commit(move_id) {
  wal.append({move_id, state: COMMITTED});
  notify(recipient, {move_id, amount});
}
// recipient
on_notify(move) {
  if (!wal.has(move.move_id)) wal.append({move.move_id, state: RECEIVED});
  apply_credits(move.tenant, move.amount);
  wal.append({move.move_id, state: APPLIED});
  ack(move.move_id);
}
ack(move_id) => donor wal.append({move_id, state: ACKED});
```

maya singh: we need the API semantics for can_reweight(tenant): should return reasons like "PINS_ACTIVE", "QOS_EXEMPT", or "OK". also a best-effort hint "suggested_retry_after_secs" so reconcilers don't spin.

kevin park: what about concurrent donors targeting the same recipient (hot arrival). must bound throughput: cap outstanding escrow moves per-tenant per-slab to avoid credit flood. also exponential backoff on retries.

noah: agreed. cap = min(8, tenant_shard_count) + dynamic step-up during true spike.

leo wang: safety invariant to write into RFC: "escrowed + committed + available == tenant_total_credits" at all times (accounting). auditors should be able to prove that invariant by WAL scan.

tara gomez: rollout: 1) simulate in staging with deterministic WAL crashes and verify invariant, 2) canary small fraction of slabs in prod with telemetry, 3) ramp with ops oncall until stable. also record rollback playbook.

sre-bot: alert candidate created: "escrow_entries_stuck" (severity: p1) if any escrow entry > 5m, and "escrow_commit_failure_rate" > 0.5% over 5m.

noah: next steps: I'll write a short RFC with protocol states, WAL schema, idempotency guarantees, can_reweight API, metrics table, and canary plan. cc: @maya for QoS policy and @leo for ops playbook.

maya singh: ack, will add QoS exemptions and emergency opt-out semantics.

kevin park: pairing with you on implementation spike for WAL-backed escrow and idempotency this week.

noah: thanks all. keeping this thread as the design brain dump, will convert to RFC tomorrow. :)
